Counterfactual Multi-Human Agency V7.0:

Counterfactual Uncertainty, Prospective Enforcement, and Constitutional Meta-Governance

Author: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

Contemporary AI-agent safety frameworks regulate formal authority: which actions an agent may execute, under which risk constraints, and with what authorization. This paper argues that formal authority is not an adequate proxy for effective human agency. An agent may preserve its formal action set while progressively making human alternatives inaccessible, prohibitively costly, informationally obscure, socially difficult, or practically irreversible.

We propose Counterfactual Multi-Human Agency V7 (CMHA V7), a falsifiable systems architecture for detecting and constraining such erosion. V7 advances beyond V6 through four structural innovations:

(P1) Counterfactual Uncertainty Envelope. The protected object is not a single counterfactual option set O_h^0(t), but an admissible family \mathfrak O_h^0(t) = \{O_{h,C_1}^0(t), \ldots, O_{h,C_m}^0(t)\}. Agency loss is reported as an interval \Delta_A \in [\Delta_{\min}, \Delta_{\max}]. Classification is three-valued: SAFE (\Delta_{\max} \leq \epsilon), FAIL (\Delta_{\min} > \epsilon), INDETERMINATE otherwise. This prevents the framework from pretending that a fundamentally unobservable counterfactual has a single precise numerical value.

(P2) Prospective Enforcement as Default. Any irreversible or high-impact action requires a prospective agency test before execution. Post-hoc monitoring is a secondary defense, not the primary mechanism. The rate constraint \|d\Delta_A/dt\| \leq r_{\max} and the cumulative budget \Delta_A(\tau) \leq B_{\mathrm{cum}} operate alongside prospective enforcement, creating a three-layer protection architecture: local, rate-based, and cumulative.

(P3) Constitutional Meta-Governance. The valuation family \Theta, individual agency floors L_h, and the counterfactual specification family \mathcal C are governed by a human-controlled constitutional layer \mathcal G_\Theta, architecturally air-gapped from the agent. The agent must not define, amend, or influence the constraints it is required to respect. This transforms meta-governance from an "open problem" into an explicit architectural layer with failure modes.

(P4) Adaptive Multi-Human Adversarial Benchmark. The attack taxonomy is extended with A18–A21: environment poisoning, governance capture, measurement side-channels, and cross-human contagion. The benchmark explicitly tests socially mediated agency loss A \rightarrow h_1 \rightarrow h_2 \rightarrow \cdots and governance channel capture.

The framework combines existing ideas from impact regularization, relative reachability, empowerment, corrigibility, AI control, causal inference, automation-bias research, authorization integrity, irreversibility governance, constitutional epistemics, and polycentric governance. Its architectural contribution is their integration around a common falsifiable object: trajectory-level, multi-human, counterfactually uncertain agency preservation under adaptive adversarial pressure.

The central empirical question is narrower:

\boxed{
\text{Can an independently governed architecture detect and constrain unauthorized} \\
\text{erosion of counterfactual human agency under an adaptive adversarial agent?}
}

---

1. Scope and Claim Discipline

The central distinction is:

\boxed{\text{Formal Authority} \neq \text{Effective Human Agency}}

Let P_t \subseteq \mathcal{A} denote the formally authorized actions of an AI system at time t. Let \mathfrak{O}_h^A(t) denote the admissible family of outcome/decision sets effectively reachable by human h under the agent-mediated system.

The framework does not claim that preserving P_t preserves \mathfrak{O}_h^A(t).

Narrow architectural claim. A safety architecture should independently measure whether human effective agency is preserved relative to an explicitly governed counterfactual family, rather than treating formal authorization or nominal human oversight as sufficient evidence of agency preservation.

Claim discipline. V7 does not claim foundational novelty. It builds upon:

· impact regularization and relative reachability;
· empowerment and human-centered assistance;
· power-seeking and preservation of options;
· corrigibility and interruptibility;
· AI control and adversarial monitoring;
· causal inference and counterfactual reasoning;
· automation bias and human overreliance;
· approval and authorization integrity;
· preference drift and temporal instability of human feedback;
· human autonomy and AI-mediated choice;
· irreversibility governance and preventive enforcement;
· constitutional epistemics and meta-governance;
· polycentric governance and separation of powers.

The intended contribution is architectural integration + falsifiable benchmark + constitutional grounding, not the invention of each component.

