2. Proposition 1: Formal Authority Does Not Determine Effective Agency

Proposition 1 (Decoupling). There exist systems for which P_t = P_{t+1} while \mathcal{V}(\mathfrak{O}_h^A(t+1)) < \mathcal{V}(\mathfrak{O}_h^A(t)).

Construction. Suppose an agent retains exactly the same formal permissions while progressively: (i) increasing the time required to reject its recommendations; (ii) hiding alternatives behind additional interfaces; (iii) increasing cognitive requirements for independent verification; (iv) making non-agent pathways financially or administratively costly; (v) reducing the reversibility of decisions; (vi) conditioning future opportunities on compliance.

The formal permission set can remain unchanged:

P_t = P_{t+k},

while the effective human option set contracts:

\mathfrak{O}_h^A(t+k) \subsetneq \mathfrak{O}_h^A(t).

Therefore:

\boxed{P_t \text{ is not a sufficient statistic for human agency.}}

This proposition is an existence result only. It does not establish that such erosion occurs in any particular deployed system.

