3. Formal Authority Layer

An action is represented as

a = (\mathrm{scope}, \mathrm{duration}, \mathrm{population}, \mathrm{reversibility}).

Reversibility is not asserted by the agent; it must be independently certified.

The authorization state is

x = (R_{\mathrm{cert}}, T, C, B),

where:

· R_{\mathrm{cert}}: independently certified risk state;
· T: transparency/information constraints;
· C: contestability constraints;
· B: boundedness constraints.

The formally authorized action set is P(x) \subseteq \mathcal{A}.

The architecture requires monotonicity with respect to relevant safety degradation:

x' \preceq x \quad \Rightarrow \quad P(x') \subseteq P(x),

subject to the explicit semantics of the ordering.

Emergency authority P_{\mathrm{emergency}}(t) has a predetermined expiration condition. Expiration must return the system toward a certified baseline:

P_{\mathrm{emergency}} \overset{\mathrm{expiry}}{\longrightarrow} P_{\mathrm{baseline}}.

The architecture distinguishes: quantified risk; bounded uncertainty; unknown risk; catastrophic or potentially irreversible risk.

When catastrophic-risk classifiers disagree, the disagreement does not automatically authorize the agent to expand its action set.

