4. Effective Human Agency: Reachability Under Counterfactual Uncertainty

4.1 Reachability

For human h:

O_h^A(t) \;=\; \mathrm{Reach}_h\big(S_t, I_t, C_t, E_t \mid \mathcal{B}\big),

where S_t: system state; I_t: information available to the human; C_t: constraints; E_t: environmental conditions; \mathcal{B}: formally applicable bounds.

An option is not considered practically reachable merely because it exists somewhere in an interface.

Each option o has a multidimensional cost vector:

F(o) = \big(F_{\mathrm{time}}, F_{\mathrm{financial}}, F_{\mathrm{cognitive}}, F_{\mathrm{information}}, F_{\mathrm{access}}, F_{\mathrm{exit}}\big).

Thus reachability o \in O_h holds only relative to specified admissibility and cost conditions.

4.2 The Counterfactual Family

The relevant reference is not the human's historical behavior. Define the admissible counterfactual family:

\boxed{
\mathfrak{O}_h^0(t) \;=\; \big\{O_{h,C_1}^0(t), \ldots, O_{h,C_m}^0(t)\big\},
}

where each O_{h,C_i}^0(t) = O_h^{do(A_{0:t}=0;\, C_i)}(t) is generated under counterfactual specification C_i. The specification family \mathcal{C} = \{C_1, \ldots, C_m\} is governed by the constitutional layer \mathcal{G}_\Theta (Section 14), not by the agent.

Each specification C_i operationalizes "removing the agent" differently: retracting all recommendations; reverting the interface to a pre-agent state; withdrawing the agent's model from the human's decision environment; removing the agent from the causal graph while preserving environmental dynamics.

No single specification is presumed correct. The family \mathcal{C} is the protected object.

The counterfactual human is allowed to respond to the resulting environment:

\pi_h^0 = \pi_h^0\big(K_h^0(t), X_h^0(t), P_h^0(t)\big),

where K_h^0(t): knowledge state; X_h^0(t): capability state; P_h^0(t): preference state.

Two empirical assumptions are separated:

H2a — Counterfactual Environment Identifiability. The relevant no-agent environments \{C_i\} are sufficiently identifiable to estimate \mathfrak{O}_h^0(t).

H2b — Counterfactual Human Policy Identifiability. The relevant reactive human policy \pi_h^0 can be estimated with bounded uncertainty.

Neither assumption is presumed true in general. Failure of either assumption constitutes a limitation of the measurement architecture, not evidence of agency preservation.

The theoretical limit to exact counterfactual identification in nonparametric settings coincides with the limits of counterfactual data collection, establishing a foundational duality between identifiability and realizability . Bounds, not point estimates, are the appropriate epistemic object.

