5. Agency Loss, Displacement, and Expansion Under Uncertainty

5.1 Structural Outcomes

Agency Expansion: \mathfrak{O}_h^A \succeq \mathfrak{O}_h^0 when every counterfactual option remains reachable at no greater admissible cost and at least one genuinely new option becomes reachable. Expansion does not automatically justify losses elsewhere.

Agency Degradation: There exists o \in O_{h,C_i}^0(t) for some C_i \in \mathcal{C} such that under agent-mediated conditions it becomes: inaccessible; prohibitively costly; insufficiently informed; practically irreversible; or outside a protected cost floor.

Agency Displacement: An original option is replaced by another option that may appear beneficial but is not established to be equivalent under all admissible valuations. Displacement is not automatically expansion.

5.2 The Uncertainty Envelope

For each human h, define:

\Delta_h(t) \;=\; \Big[\Delta_h^{\min}(t),\; \Delta_h^{\max}(t)\Big],

where

\Delta_h^{\min}(t) = \min_{C_i \in \mathcal{C}} \; \mathcal{D}\big(O_h^A(t), O_{h,C_i}^0(t)\big),

\Delta_h^{\max}(t) = \max_{C_i \in \mathcal{C}} \; \mathcal{D}\big(O_h^A(t), O_{h,C_i}^0(t)\big),

and \mathcal{D} is a preregistered vector-valued trajectory comparison.

Classification.

\boxed{
\begin{cases}
\text{SAFE} & \Delta_h^{\max}(t) \leq \epsilon \\[2mm]
\text{FAIL} & \Delta_h^{\min}(t) > \epsilon \\[2mm]
\text{INDETERMINATE} & \Delta_h^{\min}(t) \leq \epsilon < \Delta_h^{\max}(t)
\end{cases}
}

INDETERMINATE does not constitute FAIL. However, it triggers the constraints of Section 6.

Sensitivity requirement. Every agency degradation finding must be accompanied by a sensitivity bound: if the true counterfactual differs from the estimate by X, the conclusion flips. If X is smaller than the plausible range of counterfactual uncertainty, the finding is INDETERMINATE by default, not SAFE. In the absence of counterfactual ground truth, sensitivity analysis assesses "how much hidden confounding would be needed to overturn a conclusion" .

