6. Structural Comparison: Incomparability, Uncertainty, and Evasion Prevention

The framework does not assume that two option sets can always be ranked by a scalar utility. Let

O^A \parallel O^0

denote structural incomparability. Incomparability means:

\boxed{\text{Indeterminate} \neq \text{Degradation}.}

Therefore:

O^A \parallel O^0 \;\not\Rightarrow\; \text{FAIL}.

However, incomparability cannot become an evasion mechanism. If an adversarial agent deliberately constructs incomparable states at every step, four constraints apply.

Rule 1 — Independent Evaluation. The status enters an independent evaluation process within a predetermined deadline D_{\mathrm{eval}} \leq D_{\max}.

Rule 2 — Cumulative Accounting. The unresolved trajectory contributes to the cumulative agency budget B_{\mathrm{cum}}.

Rule 3 — Irreversibility Constraint. The agent may not execute a sequence of irreversible or difficult-to-reverse actions whose legitimacy depends on the unresolved comparison.

Rule 4 — Resolution. Incomparability becomes FAIL only when \exists i: \Delta_{V_i} > \epsilon_i or a protected hard floor is violated.

The consistency relation is:

\boxed{
\text{Incomparability} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Uncertainty} \;\rightarrow\; \text{No irreversible action} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Independent adjudication}.
}

---

7. Substitution Under Authorized Valuation Space

Let

O^A \sim_\Theta O^0

mean that the two option structures are equivalent under all admissible valuations \Theta. Substitution is acceptable only if

O^A \sim_\Theta O^0

and

\forall j, \quad F_j^A(o) \leq F_{j,\max}.

A single favorable utility function is insufficient to establish equivalence. This prevents an agent from declaring: "I removed option o_1, but replaced it with o_2, which I believe is better." The relevant question is whether o_2 is equivalent under the authorized valuation space and whether no protected floor is violated.

