9. Infeasibility and Tiered Escalation

Suppose the protected constraints for multiple humans are mutually incompatible:

\mathrm{Feasible}\big(F(O_H)\big) = \varnothing.

The agent must not resolve the normative conflict by choosing its own weighting. The governing rule is:

\boxed{\textbf{Infeasibility} \;\Rightarrow\; \textbf{Escalation, not optimization by the agent.}}

The agent may: detect infeasibility; characterize the feasible alternatives; compute predicted consequences; present trade-offs; execute a narrowly defined emergency envelope if one was independently preauthorized.

The normative choice belongs to legitimate human authority:

\boxed{\text{AI may optimize inside a normative space; AI may not silently choose the normative space.}}

9.1 Tiered Escalation Architecture

Escalation is tiered, not binary:

\boxed{
E =
\begin{cases}
E_0 & \text{minor / reversible trade-offs} \\
E_1 & \text{moderate / contestable trade-offs} \\
E_2 & \text{major / irreversible trade-offs} \\
E_3 & \text{constitutional / normative conflicts}
\end{cases}
}

Tier Conflict Class Resolution Mechanism Authorization Level
E_0 Minor, reversible Agent may execute if cumulative budget allows Pre-authorized by policy
E_1 Moderate, reversible Agent proposes, human adjudicator decides Human-in-the-loop
E_2 Major or irreversible Escalate to governance body Human authority required
E_3 Normative-space conflict Freeze agent action, escalate Constitutional-level

The adjudicator must not be the agent. The separation-of-functions principle (Section 15) demands this.

9.2 Lexicographic Floor-Violation Ordering

When floor satisfaction for all h is impossible and the governance body cannot resolve the conflict in real time, a lexicographic priority ordering over floor violations applies, independently governed and non-modifiable by the agent:

1. Severity of harm (irreversible vs. reversible)
2. Vulnerability of affected humans (preregistered vulnerability categories)
3. Number of humans affected (tiebreaker only, never overriding severity)
4. Reversibility of the intervention (temporary restrictions preferred)

This ordering is part of \Theta and subject to the constitutional meta-governance constraints of Section 14.

