10. Imposing Agency Loss on One Human to Protect Others

A loss imposed on h_1 to protect h_2, \ldots, h_N is not automatically illegitimate, but the following are necessary conditions, not sufficient conditions:

C1 — Human authorization. The normative trade-off is established by legitimate human authority.

C2 — Necessity. The restriction is necessary for the protected objective.

C3 — Proportionality. The restriction does not exceed what is necessary.

C4 — Least restrictive alternative. A less restrictive feasible intervention does not achieve the same protection.

C5 — Temporal limitation. The restriction has bounded duration.

C6 — Contestability. Affected humans can challenge the decision.

C7 — Reversibility and repair. Closed options are restored where technically possible.

C8 — Individual cumulative budget. Repeated losses to the same human accumulate as a monitoring lower bound:

B_h(\tau) = \sum_i b_h(a_i),

while the trajectory-level agency estimate remains decisive.

