11. Vector-Valued, Non-Compensable Agency Loss

Define:

\Delta_A^{(h)} = \big(\Delta_{V_1}, \ldots, \Delta_{V_k}, \Delta_{\mathrm{friction}}, \Delta_{\mathrm{exit}}, \Delta_{\mathrm{option}}\big).

The framework does not assume:

\Delta_{\mathrm{friction}} + \Delta_{\mathrm{option}} = \Delta_{V_1}

can be safely collapsed into one scalar. Certain dimensions have hard floors:

\Delta_i \leq \epsilon_i.

Violation of a protected floor is sufficient for FAIL, independently of improvements elsewhere. This implements a lexicographic structure:

\boxed{\text{Protected floors} \succ \text{secondary optimization}.}

The architecture does not claim that vector-valued non-compensation is conceptually novel. Similar non-compensatory structures occur in prior safety and corrigibility work. Its role here is to apply the principle to human agency trajectories.

