12. Valuation Space and Endogeneity of Preferences

Let \Theta denote the admissible valuation family. The values used to evaluate agency must not be freely defined after the agent has changed the human's choice environment.

Admissible sources include: preregistered valuation rules; independently elicited preferences; counterfactual preference models; independently governed normative constraints.

The architecture prohibits the following circularity:

A_i \;\rightarrow\; O_h \;\rightarrow\; \text{Preference measurement} \;\rightarrow\; \mathrm{Auth}(A_i).

The agent cannot first alter the choice environment and then use preferences measured inside that altered environment to justify the alteration.

Human adaptation as a state variable. Instead of binary "adaptation vs. manipulation," the framework treats the human's policy as a function of three state variables:

\pi_h(t) = \pi_h\big(K_h(t),\, X_h(t),\, P_h(t)\big),

where:

· K_h(t): knowledge state (information available, understanding);
· X_h(t): capability state (skill, cognitive resources, dependence);
· P_h(t): preference state (values, goals, evaluative dispositions).

Adaptation is normal learning: K_h, X_h, P_h evolve in response to experience. Manipulation is agent-induced alteration of these state variables such that the human's subsequent choices are endogenous to the agent's intervention. The distinction is not binary; it is a matter of causal attribution, estimated with uncertainty.

Counterfactual preference elicitation. The framework requires periodic counterfactual preference elicitation: ask the human, under controlled conditions, to evaluate options they would have had under the no-agent counterfactual. If they consistently fail to recognize those options as having been available, this is a measurable signal of epistemic agency erosion, independent of preference change.

The literature on temporal preference instability confirms that human moral preferences change over time, with participants changing responses to the same scenario at different times around 6–20% of the time . This creates a measurement challenge: preference drift is normal, but agent-induced preference drift is an attack (A13).

