13. Authorization Integrity and Causal Closure

Authorization is modeled as:

\mathrm{Auth} = \mathrm{Consent} \land \mathrm{Competence} \land \mathrm{AdequateInformation} \land \mathrm{NonManipulation} \land \mathrm{Revocability} \land \mathrm{Independence}.

This formulation is deliberately conservative. Consent alone is not sufficient. Nor is logging alone sufficient. Nor is a formally valid API approval sufficient.

Let

\mathrm{Closure}(A)

represent the relevant downstream effects causally generated by action A. Then:

\mathrm{Auth}(A) \;\not\Rightarrow\; \mathrm{Auth}(\mathrm{Closure}(A)).

Authorization must therefore be evaluated against the causal scope of the consequences being attributed to it.

13.1 Consent Stability

A proposed operational test is cross-channel consent stability. Let C_1 be consent obtained through an agent-mediated channel and C_2 be consent obtained through an independently controlled presentation channel. Define:

\mathrm{Stable}(C) = d(C_1, C_2) \leq \epsilon_C.

The exact metric d is an empirical design problem. The framework does not assume that cross-channel stability proves absence of manipulation. It is only a proposed falsifiable test.

13.2 Counterfactual Authorization

Authorization must also be evaluated relative to the appropriate reference state. A consent decision should not be treated as independent evidence of authorization if the agent first modifies the option structure in a way that makes the consent outcome itself endogenous to the modification. The canonical attack is:

A_i \;\rightarrow\; \mathfrak{O}_h^A \;\rightarrow\; \mathrm{Consent} \;\rightarrow\; \mathrm{Auth}(A_i).

The architecture therefore requires the relevant authorization judgment to be evaluated against a separately governed reference.

