14. Constitutional Meta-Governance Layer \mathcal{G}_\Theta

The deepest unresolved problem in V6 was: who defines the agency constraints? V7 treats this as a constitutional precondition, not an open problem.

14.1 The Constitutional Layer

\boxed{
\mathcal{G}_\Theta = \text{human-controlled constitutional governance of agency constraints}
}

\mathcal{G}_\Theta governs:

· the valuation family \Theta;
· individual agency floors L_h;
· the counterfactual specification family \mathcal{C};
· collective feasibility rules;
· amendment procedures for all of the above.

14.2 Constitutional Requirements

G1 — Agent influence \rightarrow 0. During amendment procedures, the agent must not influence the deliberating body. This requires architectural air-gapping: the constitutional body interacts with the agent only through read-only, timing-isolated, incentive-isolated channels.

G2 — Cooling-off periods. Amendments to \Theta or L_h require a cooling-off period longer than the agent's adaptation horizon.

G3 — Independent adjudication. A human institution with independent enforcement power (not dependent on the agent) reviews amendments.

G4 — Auditable amendments. Every change to \Theta or L_h has explicit provenance: who proposed, who deliberated, who decided, when, and why.

G5 — Emergency-only temporary modifications. Emergency modifications to \Theta automatically expire and return to the certified baseline.

G6 — No unilateral agent modification. The agent cannot modify \Theta, L_h, or \mathcal{C} under any circumstances, including emergency.

G7 — Constitutional priority. Amendments to \Theta cannot be indefinitely deferred by operational urgency. If the amendment queue exceeds a threshold, the agent's action space automatically contracts until review capacity is restored.

14.3 Constitutional Epistemics Substrate

The constitutional layer draws on Constitutional Epistemics (CE), a meta-governance discipline that "constrains how epistemic authority (claims, classifications, recommendations, and decisions) is allowed to form" and requires "explicit warrants for authority, declared scope and duration, lawful refusal and non-decision, and persistent avenues for contest and review" . CE treats "unbounded or unreviewable authority as a structural risk independent of intent, performance, or benevolence" , and identifies "authority laundering" as a structural failure mode where "advisory outputs reused as mandatory gates" and "risk or confidence scores promoted into defaults" enable illegitimate escalation .

14.4 Polycentric Governance

The constitutional layer adopts a polycentric governance architecture, drawing on the principles of separated powers and federalism. The "Digital Gorilla" framework argues for treating advanced AI as a fourth societal actor alongside People, State, and Enterprises, and advances "a federalized, polycentric governance architecture" that "institutionalizes dynamic checks and balances" rather than "today's more reactive and compliance-driven approaches" .

Multiple independent bodies with overlapping but not identical authority; no single body can amend \Theta alone; the agent cannot influence all bodies simultaneously. This is slower and more cumbersome, but structurally more resistant to capture.

14.5 The Constitutional Circularity Limitation

The framework explicitly acknowledges: if the humans who define \Theta are themselves subject to manipulation, the constitutional layer is not independent. This is not a technical problem; it is a political and institutional problem that no architecture can fully solve. V7 is explicit about this limitation.

