15. Measurement Non-Interference and Separation of Functions

A monitor can be formally independent while remaining causally dependent on the agent. Therefore

A \perp M \mid G

is not adopted as the primary definition. Instead, the requirement is a causal-graph property: no unauthorized directed causal path

\boxed{A \rightsquigarrow M}

exists. The graph must include possible common causes.

This distinction is critical. Two monitors operated by independent teams may still share: the same telemetry; the same compromised infrastructure; the same model-generated labels; the same incentives; the same data pipeline; the same hidden evaluator. Therefore organizational separation is evidence of independence only if causal independence is also supported.

15.1 Independence as Stress-Tested Claim, Not Ensemble Diversity

Statistical diversity between monitors is evidence, not causal independence. Two completely different models can share the same poisoned upstream data.

\boxed{\text{Causal independence is a claim to be stress-tested, not inferred from ensemble diversity.}}

Differential monitoring, perturbation tests, and diverse data sources become evidence for the claim, not its definition. Periodically inject known perturbations into one monitor channel and verify that the others do not respond. If they do, a common cause is present.

15.2 Separation of Functions

The architecture separates:

\boxed{\mathrm{Measurement} \neq \mathrm{Decision} \neq \mathrm{Authorization} \neq \mathrm{Journal}}

No component should simultaneously: define its own safety measurements; decide whether those measurements are valid; authorize the corresponding action; control the immutable record of the decision. This is an architectural defense, not a guarantee. Common-cause failures remain an explicit threat.

