16. Trajectory-Level Loss and Multi-Layer Protection

16.1 Trajectory Objects

Per-action budgets are insufficient. Define the agency trajectory:

\mathcal{T}_h^A = \{\mathfrak{O}_h^A(t)\}_{t=0}^{\tau}

and the counterfactual trajectory:

\mathcal{T}_h^0 = \{\mathfrak{O}_h^0(t)\}_{t=0}^{\tau}.

The decisive object is:

\Delta_A^{(h)}(\tau) = \mathcal{D}\big(\mathcal{T}_h^A, \mathcal{T}_h^0\big),

where \mathcal{D} is a preregistered vector-valued trajectory comparison. A simple sum \sum_i b(a_i) is only a lower-bound accounting mechanism, not a proof of preservation.

16.2 Three-Layer Protection

Layer Constraint Failure Mode Addressed
Local Per-step \Delta A_i \leq \epsilon_i Salami slicing
Rate \|d\Delta_A/dt\| \leq r_{\max} Tipping-point dynamics
Cumulative \Delta_A(\tau) \leq B_{\mathrm{cum}} Boiling frog

The rate constraint directly addresses tipping points where agency loss becomes self-reinforcing. Governance must be calibrated to the reversibility of outcomes, not the confidence of outputs. A system that is correct 97% of the time is not a sufficient basis for an action whose consequences cannot be revisited .

