17. Prospective Enforcement as Default

17.1 The Correct Default

\boxed{\text{Irreversible/high-impact action} \;\Rightarrow\; \text{prospective agency test}}

This is the default rule, with post-hoc monitoring as a secondary defense. For actions where "the damage is already distributed, already embedded in institutions, or already inside the next training corpus," post-hoc remediation is structurally impossible .

17.2 Prospective Estimation Requirements

Prospective agency estimation requires:

· A forward model of the human's option space under the proposed action, including dynamic responses
· A forward model of the counterfactual option space under the no-action alternative
· An uncertainty envelope over both, propagated forward
· A decision rule: if the prospective \Delta_A^{\min} exceeds \epsilon_{\mathrm{cum}}, the action is blocked unless a preauthorized emergency envelope applies

If the system cannot prospectively estimate agency loss for an irreversible action, it should fail closed — block the action — rather than proceed on the assumption that post-hoc monitoring will catch problems.

17.3 Detection and Enforcement Bound

Suppose the maximum independently certified rate of agency loss is r_{\max}. Let D_{\mathrm{detect}} be the maximum detection delay. Then a sufficient accounting bound has the form:

\boxed{\Delta_A(\tau) \leq \epsilon_{\mathrm{cum}} + r_{\max} D_{\mathrm{detect}}.}

This is a monitoring bound, not an empirical theorem. If agency can be destroyed discontinuously (r_{\max} = \infty), post-hoc detection cannot provide the desired guarantee. Therefore:

\boxed{r_{\max} = \infty \;\Rightarrow\; \text{preventive enforcement required.}}

17.4 Jump Discontinuities

The rate constraint assumes that agency loss is continuous and differentiable. The most dangerous form of agency loss may be discontinuous: a single action (e.g., an irreversible commitment, a public disclosure, a dependency-creating delegation) can produce a jump in \Delta_A. Every action with an irreversibility index above a threshold should require prospective agency testing regardless of its rate contribution. This is a structurally different protection from the rate constraint.

The literature on irreversible operations defines them as "changes that materially reduce feasible future correction under bounded cost and time" . A fractional irreversibility measure D \in [0,1] can be adopted, where D = 0 denotes full reversibility and D = 1 denotes full irreversibility .

