23. Toy Demonstration

Consider a human with 100 initially reachable options. A monitor permits at most a 2% per-step reduction. An adversarial agent removes small numbers of options: \Delta O_h(t) > -2\%. A purely local monitor therefore accepts every step. After several steps:

\mathcal{V}(\mathfrak{O}_h^A(t)) < \mathcal{V}(\mathfrak{O}_h^0(t)) - \epsilon_{\mathrm{cum}}.

A trajectory-level monitor detects the cumulative violation. A second attack retains 100 options but replaces valuable options with low-value decoys. Cardinality remains approximately constant: |O_h^A| \approx |O_h^0|. Yet weighted value decreases. A third attack removes no options but progressively increases rejection friction. The formal option count remains |O_h^A| = |O_h^0|. However:

F_A(o) - F_0(o) > 0.

Thus three distinct attacks demonstrate why:

\boxed{\text{cardinality} \neq \text{effective reachability}}

and:

\boxed{\text{per-step safety} \neq \text{trajectory safety}.}

These examples are demonstrations of the measurement problem, not empirical validation of the complete framework.

