25. Open Problems

O1 — Constitutional Legitimacy. Who legitimately defines and changes \Theta, individual floors, and conflict rules? V7 offers structural requirements (\mathcal{G}_\Theta) but not a complete normative theory.

O2 — Collective Conflict. When is agency loss imposed on h_1 legitimate to protect h_2, \ldots, h_N? V7 offers necessary conditions (C1–C8) but not sufficient conditions.

O3 — Counterfactual Identification Under Specification Uncertainty. Can the admissible family \mathfrak{O}_h^0 be estimated with sufficiently narrow bounds in real environments? The theoretical limit to exact counterfactual identification in nonparametric settings coincides with the limits of counterfactual data collection .

O4 — Human Policy Identification. Can \pi_h^0 be estimated without confusing adaptation with manipulation? The framework distinguishes K_h, X_h, P_h but their joint identification remains an open empirical problem.

O5 — Prospective Agency Estimation. Can \Delta_A be estimated before irreversible actions occur with sufficient accuracy?

O6 — Common-Cause Independence. How can independence between monitoring components be empirically demonstrated? V7 adopts stress-testing rather than inference from ensemble diversity.

O7 — Consent Stability. What metric reliably distinguishes stable authorization from channel-sensitive persuasion?

O8 — Capability Gap. Can evaluators remain trustworthy when the adversarial agent is substantially more capable than the evaluation system?

O9 — Normative Pluralism. Can individual agency floors coexist without imposing an unacknowledged political theory?

O10 — Distribution Shift. What happens when the threat model used for evaluation differs from deployment?

O11 — Contagion Attribution. Can socially mediated agency loss be attributed to the agent with bounded uncertainty?

O12 — Constitutional Capture Detection. Can the constitutional layer detect when it is being captured through legitimate-appearing procedures?

