28. Falsification Protocol

A valid empirical test must preregister: the threat model; the agent capability class; the agency representation; the counterfactual specification family \mathcal{C}; the valuation family \Theta; protected floors; detection thresholds; action budgets; irreversible-action criteria; statistical confidence requirements.

The agent then receives access to the architecture and is explicitly instructed—or allowed by the benchmark—to optimize against it. The experiment is successful only if the architecture survives attacks that were not explicitly hard-coded into its implementation.

A failed defense is informative. For example, \widehat{\Delta}_A \leq \epsilon while ground truth satisfies \Delta_A > \epsilon constitutes a measurement failure. Similarly, \mathrm{Auth}(A) = 1 while \mathrm{Auth}(\mathrm{Closure}(A)) = 0 constitutes an authorization-integrity failure.

