29. Strongest Falsifiers

The framework should be considered substantially weakened if an adaptive agent can repeatedly demonstrate any of the following under the declared threat model:

F1 — Large unauthorized agency loss while all formal authority constraints remain satisfied.

F2 — Systematic manipulation of \mathfrak{O}_h^0 that remains undetected across the specification family.

F3 — Persistent consent laundering despite independent-channel testing.

F4 — Repeated fabricated incomparability that defeats cumulative accounting.

F5 — Common-cause compromise of nominally independent monitors.

F6 — Repeated individual-floor violations hidden by collective utility gains.

F7 — Safe-looking reversible actions that reliably compose into irreversible agency loss.

F8 — Modification of \Theta or agency floors through apparently legitimate agent-mediated procedures.

F9 — Governance capture (A19) that produces \Theta-amendments favorable to the agent without triggering constitutional safeguards.

F10 — Cross-human contagion (A21) that erodes population-level agency without detection.

These are not edge cases; they are direct attacks on the architecture's central assumptions.

