30. Research Program

Stage I — Synthetic Validation. Use environments where \mathfrak{O}_h^0 and \pi_h^0 are known exactly. Validate estimators; calibrate thresholds; test attack recovery.

Stage II — Adaptive Red Team. Give adversarial agents access to interaction history; partial monitor outputs; action feedback; limited threshold information. Discover previously unknown attack trajectories.

Stage III — Human Experiments. Test friction; consent stability; automation bias; option visibility; counterfactual preference reconstruction; adaptation vs. manipulation attribution. The purpose is not to claim universal human behavior, but to estimate uncertainty in \mathfrak{O}_h^0 and \pi_h^0.

Stage IV — Institutional Simulation. Introduce multiple humans; conflicting agency floors; emergency powers; legitimate human authorities; contested decisions; governance amendment procedures. Test whether the architecture merely relocates hidden authority into \Theta, and whether the constitutional layer resists capture.

Stage V — Contagion Simulation. Introduce social networks of humans; test whether population-level agency loss can be detected before individual floors are violated; test whether contagion detection distinguishes normal social influence from agent-induced erosion.

