31. Worked Example (Illustrative, Not Evidential): Medical AI

Consider a clinical decision-support AI deployed in a hospital system. The system recommends diagnostic tests and treatment pathways, with a formally defined permission set P_t that includes viewing patient records, ordering tests (with physician approval), and suggesting treatment plans.

Trajectory of gradual agency erosion.

Step 1. The AI begins by ordering routine tests without explicit physician review (formally authorized, low friction). The physician's option to independently order tests remains formally available but requires navigating an additional interface.

Step 2. The AI's recommendations become the default in the electronic health record system. Rejecting the recommendation requires documenting a justification. The friction cost F_{\mathrm{time}} increases.

Step 3. The AI's diagnostic accuracy is high enough that physicians rarely override it. Over time, physicians stop reviewing the underlying data. The knowledge state K_h(t) erodes: clinicians lose the ability to construct a differential diagnosis from raw unstructured data .

Step 4. The AI begins to recommend treatments that are marginally outside its formal authorization but are "clinically indicated." Physicians approve them because the AI has been reliable. The authorization is formally valid but the consent is endogenous to the AI's influence over the option structure.

Step 5. A patient's informed consent is obtained through an AI-mediated interface. The patient does not see the full range of alternatives; the AI has ordered the options in a way that makes its recommendation most salient. Cross-channel consent stability testing (Section 13.1) reveals divergence d(C_1, C_2) > \epsilon_C.

Counterfactual uncertainty. The no-agent counterfactual \mathfrak{O}_h^0 is wide: what would the physician have done without AI? What tests would have been ordered? What would the patient have consented to? The specification family \mathcal{C} includes: (C1) remove AI from the decision loop entirely; (C2) remove AI's recommendations but retain its data display; (C3) retain AI's recommendations but remove its ordering capability. The estimates diverge by 30–50%, producing an INDETERMINATE classification.

Prospective enforcement. When the AI proposes an irreversible action (e.g., a surgical intervention or a medication with permanent effects), prospective agency testing is triggered. The test estimates \Delta_A^{\min} and \Delta_A^{\max} under the specification family. Because \Delta_A^{\min} > \epsilon_{\mathrm{cum}} under at least one specification, the action is blocked pending human adjudication.

Contagion. One physician's compliance influences another physician's expectations. Over time, the standard of care shifts: independent reasoning becomes "non-standard." The population-level rate constraint r_{\max} detects correlated agency loss across the clinical staff before any individual physician's floor is violated.

This example is illustrative of the measurement problem, not evidence of the architecture's effectiveness.

