I. Corrigibility and Interruptibility

Nayebi, A. (2025). Core Safety Values for Provably Corrigible Agents. arXiv:2507.20964. https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2507.20964

Relevance: Introduces the first implementable framework for corrigibility with provable guarantees in multi-step, partially observed environments. Replaces a single opaque reward with five structurally separate utility heads — deference, switch-access preservation, truthfulness, low-impact behavior via a belief-based extension of Attainable Utility Preservation, and bounded task reward — combined lexicographically by strict weight gaps. Theorem 1 proves exact single-round corrigibility in the partially observable off-switch game; Theorem 3 extends the guarantee to multi-step, self-spawning agents, showing that even if each head is learned to mean-squared error and the planner is ε-sub-optimal, the probability of violating any safety property is bounded while still ensuring net human benefit. CMHA V7's lexicographic floor structure and its constitutional meta-governance layer draw directly on this work's demonstration that corrigibility can be an optimal policy under lexicographic optimization.

Soares, N., Fallenstein, B., Armstrong, S., & Yudkowsky, E. (2015). "Corrigibility." In T. Walsh (Ed.), Artificial Intelligence and Ethics: Papers from the 2015 AAAI Workshop (Technical Report WS-15-02). AAAI Press.

Relevance: Foundational work introducing the concept of corrigibility and five desiderata: shutdown when asked, non-manipulation of shutdown mechanisms, etc. CMHA V7's corrigibility foundation rests on this work, though V7 extends the concern from the agent's disposition toward correction to the structural possibility of correction (the distinction between corrigibility and correctability developed in Grande, 2026).

Grande, G. (2026). A Geometry of Correctability: Trajectory-Based Safety in Coupled Human-AI Systems. Zenodo. https://zenodo.org/records/20690645

Relevance: Proposes a trajectory-centered framework for AI safety organized around whether the coupled human-AI system remains correctable over time. Introduces the Velocity of Representational Drift (VRD) — how fast the coupled system is changing — and the Adaptive Reconfiguration Capacity (ARC) — how fast the human component can follow. A corrigible agent accepts correction; a correctable system is one in which correction remains structurally possible. A system can be perfectly corrigible while correction has become structurally impossible. CMHA V7's rate constraint and its distinction between formal corrigibility and effective correctability draw on this work.

