II. Empowerment and Reachability

Klyubin, A. S., Polani, D., & Nehaniv, C. L. (2005). "Empowerment: A Universal Agent-Centric Measure of Control." In Proceedings of the 2005 IEEE Congress on Evolutionary Computation (Vol. 1, pp. 128–135). IEEE.

Relevance: Introduces empowerment as the maximum mutual information between an agent's actions and the successor state: $\mathcal{E}(s) = \max_{p(a)} I(A; S' \mid s)$. Empowerment "evaluates a maximal number of environment states that the agent can potentially achieve by its actions" and is local, universal, and task-independent. CMHA V7 adapts this formulation to human empowerment: $\mathcal{E}_h^A(t) = \max_{p(o)} I(O_h; \text{Outcome} \mid S_t, I_t, C_t, E_t)$. The task-independent property is essential for CMHA's requirement that agency measurement not depend on the agent's stated objectives.

Salge, C., Glackin, C., & Polani, D. (2013). "Empowerment — An Introduction." arXiv:1310.1863.

Relevance: Provides a comprehensive introduction to empowerment as the information-theoretic capacity of an agent's actuation channel. The formalism grounds CMHA V7's reachability operator $\mathrm{Reach}_h(S_t, I_t, C_t, E_t \mid \mathcal{B})$ and its treatment of option reachability as a function of the human's actuation capacity relative to environmental dynamics.

