III. Impact Regularization and Relative Reachability

Krakovna, V., Orseau, L., Kumar, R., Martic, M., & Legg, S. (2019). "Penalizing Side Effects Using Stepwise Relative Reachability." arXiv:1806.01186.

Relevance: Introduces a new variant of the stepwise inaction baseline and a deviation measure based on relative reachability of states. Shows that current approaches to penalizing side effects can introduce bad incentives, e.g., to prevent any irreversible changes including actions of other agents. The relative reachability measure is sensitive to the magnitude of the agent's effects. CMHA V7 adapts this to compare the human's option space under agent mediation versus the no-agent counterfactual. The warning about perverse incentives informs CMHA's careful handling of incomparability and the prohibition on agent-defined valuation spaces.

Turner, A. M., Ratzlaff, N., & Tadepalli, P. (2020). "Avoiding Side Effects in Complex Environments." In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 33 (NeurIPS 2020).

Relevance: Scales Attainable Utility Preservation (AUP) to large environments. AUP avoids side effects by penalizing shifts in the ability to achieve randomly generated goals. "By preserving optimal value..." for a set of auxiliary objectives, the agent maintains its ability to reach many kinds of world-states. CMHA V7's counterfactual reachability comparison is a human-centered analog: rather than preserving the agent's attainable utility, it preserves the human's attainable options.

Turner, A. M. (2019/2020). "Conservative Agency via Attainable Utility Preservation." arXiv:1902.09725.

Relevance: The foundational AUP paper. Relative reachability is recovered as a special case of the AUP approach. CMHA V7 cites both relative reachability and AUP as components of the counterfactual reachability framework, noting that the two approaches are "instances of a general class of value difference measure of impact".

