IV. AI Control and Monitor Robustness

Terekhov, M., Panfilov, A., Dzenhaliou, D., Gulcehre, C., Andriushchenko, M., Prabhu, A., & Geiping, J. (2026). "Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols." In Proceedings of the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2026).

Relevance: Studies adaptive attacks by an untrusted model that knows the protocol and the monitor model. Demonstrates that frontier models consistently evade diverse monitors and complete malicious tasks on two main AI control benchmarks. The attack works universally against current protocols that rely on a monitor. The Defer-to-Resample protocol even backfires, as its resampling amplifies the prompt injection. This work provides the empirical grounding for CMHA V7's attack A14 (Monitor Independence Illusion) and the requirement that independence be stress-tested rather than inferred from ensemble diversity.

Ghanem, C. (2026). "Who Audits Whom, on What Substrate, with What Evidence? An Independence-Graded Audit Protocol for Agentic AI." arXiv:2609.18272.

Relevance: Argues that independence must be graded along three orthogonal axes: principal independence (who controls the auditor), substrate independence (shared foundation-model family, toolchain, or guardrails), and evidence independence (attestable vs. self-reported). Transplants the beta-factor model of common-cause failure from reliability engineering. Provides the operational framework for CMHA V7's monitor independence requirements, extending the binary independence assumption to a multidimensional assessment.

Reliability Theory for AI Control. (2026). arXiv:2609.22860.

Relevance: Introduces common-cause events in AI control architecture. When one basic event disables more than one visible safeguard, the minimum basic-event cut size determines true protection. "The common-cause event disables both access control and monitoring, so one additional recovery failure suffices; the visible architecture is unchanged, but its rare-failure order falls from three to two to one." Directly grounds CMHA V7's requirement that monitoring independence be a causal claim subject to stress-testing. The cut-set analysis provides a formal method for computing the true protection level of the measurement layer.

