V. Automation Bias and Human Oversight

ClinicalTrials.gov. (2026). Mitigating Automation Bias in Physician-LLM Diagnostic Reasoning Using Behavioral Nudges. NCT07328815.

Relevance: Randomized controlled trial evaluating whether behavioral nudges can reduce automation bias — "the uncritical acceptance of automated output" — in physicians using large language models for clinical decision-making. Tests a dual-mechanism behavioral nudge intervention (baseline accuracy anchoring plus case-specific color-coded confidence signals). Provides empirical grounding for CMHA V7's claim that nominal human oversight is not sufficient evidence of agency preservation. The study details how "time constraints in high-volume clinical settings create pressure to accept AI-generated recommendations without adequate scrutiny" and how "cognitive fatigue during extended shifts diminishes physicians' capacity for sustained analytical thinking".

NEJM AI. (2025). "Automation Bias in Physician-LLM Diagnostic Reasoning."

Relevance: Demonstrates "significant automation bias affecting physicians' diagnostic reasoning when using ChatGPT-4o, even when physicians are trained in AI literacy." The observed automation bias may be exacerbated by cognitive offloading, "where clinicians subconsciously reduce their cognitive effort when given an AI solution." Directly grounds CMHA V7's medical AI worked example and the knowledge state $K_h(t)$ erosion pathway.

Marchand, A.-L., Maille, N., Munoz, P., & Chaudron, L. (2024). "Confiance et biais d'automatisation : différences entre novices et experts dans un contexte militaire." Ingénierie cognitique, 7(2), 86–103.

Relevance: Studies automation bias in military pilots comparing human vs. AI-based assistance. Finds that "at equal reliability, when young participants and experts are confronted with human aid and AI-based aid, they have a preference for human aid. However, this preference is more important for experts." Challenges the invariability of automation bias and complicates the assumption that experts will reliably override harmful AI recommendations.

