VI. Authorization Integrity and Laundering

Zhang, J., Xia, H., Wu, S., Yue, J., Zhang, X., Cheng, Z., & Tu, B. (2026). "Agent Approval Laundering: Transitive Effects Beyond the Approved Invocation." arXiv:2609.28586.

Relevance: Coding-agent approval interfaces bind a human decision to a command or tool call, whereas developer tools execute the transitive workflow that invocation activates. The resulting structural record-coverage failure is termed approval laundering: "a durable record faithfully names the entry invocation yet omits effects exercised within its workflow." The paper formalizes closure-bound approval and derives an information limit: if executions with identical policy-visible fields require different effect-specific decisions, no deterministic or randomized record-only policy can guarantee both. Establishes approval laundering as "a measurable, recurrent record-coverage failure despite truthful invocation identity." CMHA V7's requirement that $\mathrm{Auth}(A) \not\Rightarrow \mathrm{Auth}(\mathrm{Closure}(A))$ and the causal closure formulation draw directly on this work.

Zhang, J., et al. (2026). "Approval Laundering: Systematizing Approval–Execution Binding Failures in AI Coding-Agent Harnesses." AI Security Portal.

Relevance: Introduces a taxonomy of six failure modes by which a harness's enforcement mechanism silently substitutes $A'$ for $A$ after approval: Scope, Argument, Temporal, Tool, Delegation, and Semantic laundering. Provides the operational taxonomy for CMHA V7's attack A9 (Authorization Laundering) and the design requirement that "before authorization, bind the entry invocation together with a source-backed prediction of the launched workflow's transitive effect boundary and its provenance".

Cerruti, T., et al. (2026). "Agent Memory Is a Surface for Endogenous Authorization Laundering." arXiv:2609.01836.

Relevance: Identifies endogenous authorization laundering where "spurious permissions written into memory lead to unauthorized actions as their provenance is washed away." Introduces EAL-Bench. Finds that "under incremental memory updates, writers create false authority for up to 50.2% of unauthorized requests; once false authority is present, executors act on it in 98.6% of trials." CMHA V7's authorization integrity framework and the requirement that authorization be evaluated against causal closure draw on this finding.

