VIII. Constitutional Meta-Governance

Mazurk, A. A. (2026). Constitutional Epistemics: A Meta-Governance Substrate for Lawful and Auditable Machine Reasoning Systems. Zenodo. https://zenodo.org/records/18114714

Relevance: Introduces Constitutional Epistemics (CE) as a governance discipline constraining "how epistemic authority (claims, classifications, recommendations, and decisions) is allowed to form, escalate, bind, and persist at runtime." CE "treats unbounded or unreviewable authority as a structural risk independent of intent, performance, or benevolence." Requires "explicit warrants for epistemic authority, declared scope and duration, lawful refusal and non-decision, and persistent avenues for contest and review." Identifies authority laundering as a structural failure mode where "advisory outputs reused as mandatory gates" and "risk scores promoted into gates" enable illegitimate escalation. "CE does not prescribe values, outcomes, or alignment objectives, nor does it replace existing legal, ethical, or risk frameworks. It functions as a protocol-level substrate." CMHA V7's constitutional layer $\mathcal{G}_\Theta$ and requirements G1–G7 draw directly on this work.

Parra-Orlandoni, M. A., Schnyder, R. A., & Mallet, C. J. (2026). "The Digital Gorilla: Rebalancing Power in the Age of AI." arXiv:2602.20080.

Relevance: Argues that existing AI governance frameworks fall into an "analogy trap" by treating AI as products, platforms, or infrastructure. Proposes treating advanced AI as a fourth societal actor alongside People, the State, and Enterprises. Advances a federalized, polycentric governance architecture that "institutionalizes dynamic checks and balances among the four actors, rather than today's more reactive and compliance-driven approaches." CMHA V7's constitutional layer adopts a polycentric governance model with multiple independent bodies, overlapping jurisdiction, and dynamic checks and balances.

Kahl, P. (2025). Redefining Democracy for the Age of AI: AI Governance and the Fiduciary Turn in the Architecture of Knowledge. PhilArchive.

Relevance: Advances a constitutional re-foundation of democracy for the age of AI, introducing Fiduciary Epistemic Governance (FEG) that "redefines AI accountability as a constitutional ethic of truth stewardship rather than risk management." Argues that "democratic legitimacy no longer rests on procedural participation or informational abundance but on fiduciary-epistemic" conditions. Provides an alternative constitutional framing for CMHA's $\mathcal{G}_\Theta$ that emphasizes the fiduciary duty of governance bodies toward affected humans.

Constitutional Causal Continuity. (2026). Zenodo.

Relevance: Presents a systems-level architecture for preserving human and institutional authority across machine reasoning, delegation, verification, execution, trajectory composition, and adaptive change in agentic AI systems. Develops the Human Reasoning Infrastructure (HRI) as a ten-module architecture. Its central thesis concerns "epistemic success and authority-bearing consequence." The architecture includes "conditional assume–guarantee closure specification, commit-time revalidation, trajectory-aware authorization, decision-environment integrity, approval-laundering controls, constitutional proxy-amendment safeguards, and reflexive recovery without machine self-legislation." CMHA V7's separation of functions and causal closure requirements are closely related to this architecture.

