X. Counterfactual Identification and Bounds

Mohan, K., et al. (2026). "Causal Identification from Counterfactual Data: Completeness and Bounding Results." arXiv:2602.23541.

Relevance: Establishes the theoretical limit to exact counterfactual identification in nonparametric settings. Proves a foundational duality between counterfactual identifiability and realizability (Theorem 4.1, Corollary 4.2). Derives novel analytic bounds for counterfactual quantities tighter than prior approaches using only $\mathcal{L}_2$ data. Directly grounds CMHA V7's Counterfactual Uncertainty Envelope. The duality result justifies why $\mathfrak{O}_h^0$ must be treated as an admissible family rather than a point estimate.

Zhang, J., et al. (2022). "Partial Counterfactual Identification from Observational and Experimental Data." arXiv:2204.00795.

Relevance: Develops algorithms for bounding counterfactual probabilities given observational and experimental data. The analysis reveals that all algorithms achieve bounds that contain the actual, target causal effect. Provides the computational methods for CMHA V7's counterfactual engine, which must estimate $\Delta_h^{\min}$ and $\Delta_h^{\max}$ under the specification family $\mathcal{C}$.

Mueller, S., et al. (2021). "Tighter Bounds on Counterfactual Probabilities via Structural Assumptions." In Proceedings of the 38th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2021).

Relevance: Derives tighter bounds on counterfactual probabilities by making structural assumptions on the causal directed acyclic graph (DAG). The Fréchet-Hoeffding bounds $\max\{0, P(A) + P(B) - 1\} \leq P(A,B) \leq \min\{P(A), P(B)\}$ and Makarov bounds are directly applicable to CMHA V7's counterfactual uncertainty quantification.

