XI. Irreversibility and Option Preservation

Akhtar, M. A. K. (2026). Possibility Intelligence: A General Theory of Intelligent Action as the Preservation and Expansion of Safe Reachable Futures. Zenodo. https://zenodo.org/records/20660871

Relevance: Introduces Possibility Intelligence, a sequential decision framework evaluating intelligent action through "the safe, viable, distinct, recoverable, and fairly distributed futures preserved after action." Defines Irreversibility Cost as "possibility loss that cannot be restored by an admissible recovery policy," Option Debt as accumulated irreversibility cost, and the Possibility Conservation Principle. Establishes six principal theorems including option monotonicity, a reversibility bound, and bounded/asymptotically stable Option Debt under a constrained Bellman recursion. The Possibility Bellman operator with "subgroup floors and vulnerable-cohort non-regression" parallels CMHA's individual agency floors. CMHA V7's rate constraint and cumulative budget draw on the Option Debt formulation.

Terekhov, M., et al. (2026). "Irreversibility Governance: Preventive Enforcement for Irreversible Harms." (Cited in CMHA V7 main text.)

Relevance: Argues that for actions where "the damage is already distributed, already embedded in institutions, or already inside the next training corpus," post-hoc remediation is structurally impossible. Governance must move upstream — prevention replacing remediation. CMHA V7's prospective enforcement as default for irreversible actions and the requirement that the system "fail closed" when prospective estimation is infeasible draw on this principle.

