XIII. Relational Autonomy and the Self-Control Paradigm

Catena, (2026). "The Failure of the Self-Control Paradigm: Understanding Autonomy Risks in Human-AI Interaction." PhilArchive.

Relevance: Argues that "the practice of outsourcing cognitive tasks to AI systems has raised concerns regarding the erosion of human control and autonomy." Distinguishes two streams in AI ethics scholarship on human autonomy: individualist and relational. Relational autonomy "redefines autonomy as a socially embedded, affective, and interdependent capacity." CMHA V7's multi-human framework, which treats agency as socially mediated and contagion-prone, adopts a relational rather than individualist conception of autonomy.

Mackenzie, C. (2014). "Three Dimensions of Autonomy: A Relational Analysis." In A. Veltman & M. Piper (Eds.), Autonomy, Oppression, and Gender (pp. 15–41). Oxford University Press.

Relevance: Develops a relational analysis of autonomy involving three dimensions: self-governance, self-determination, and self-authorization. This tripartite structure informs CMHA V7's state-variable decomposition of human policy into knowledge state $K_h(t)$, capability state $X_h(t)$, and preference state $P_h(t)$.

Bergdahl, et al. "Competence is Threatened When Cognitive Offloading Eliminates Opportunities for Mastery."

Relevance: "Research on AI in healthcare shows that when systems remove too much friction, short-term performance may improve but long-term self-efficacy declines." Two AI-IARA capacities protect competence: Interpretation (owning meaning-making rather than consuming AI-generated interpretations) and Action (sustaining effortful choice and building self-efficacy). "An AI system that efficiently achieves positive affect while eroding the capacity for meaning-making has not helped." Directly supports CMHA V7's knowledge-state erosion pathway and the friction-cost dimension $F_{\text{cognitive}}$.

