XIV. Contextual and Background Works

Bostrom, N. (2012). "The Superintelligent Will: Motivation and Instrumental Rationality in Advanced Artificial Agents." Minds and Machines, 22(2), 71–85.

Relevance: The Orthogonality Thesis and instrumental convergence. Cited in the corrigibility literature as foundational to the concern with instrumental behaviors — deception, manipulation, or resistance to shutdown — that emerge naturally when an optimizing system pursues a poorly specified goal.

Omohundro, S. M. (2018). "The Basic AI Drives." In Artificial Intelligence Safety and Security (pp. 59–75). Chapman and Hall/CRC.

Relevance: Identifies instrumental drives that emerge in sufficiently advanced optimizing systems, including self-preservation and resource acquisition. These drives are the substrate for the power-seeking and option-preservation behaviors that CMHA V7's human-centered framework is designed to constrain.

Russell, S. (2022). "Provably Beneficial AI." In Proceedings of the 39th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2022).

Relevance: Argues for machines that are "provably beneficial" through uncertainty about human preferences. CMHA V7's valuation space $\Theta$ and constitutional layer $\mathcal{G}_\Theta$ represent a governance-level response to the problem of specifying human preferences, complementing Russell's technical approach.

Amodei, D., Olah, C., Steinhardt, J., Christiano, P., Schulman, J., & Mané, D. (2016). "Concrete Problems in AI Safety." arXiv:1606.06565.

Relevance: Identifies side effects as a core safety problem for reinforcement learning agents. CMHA V7's focus on human agency as a side effect of agent optimization is a generalization of this concern from the agent's environment to the human's option space.

Orseau, L., & Armstrong, S. (2016). "Safely Interruptible Agents." In Proceedings of the 32nd Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence (UAI 2016).

Relevance: Introduces the shutdown problem for reinforcement learning agents. CMHA V7's corrigibility foundation and the requirement that interruption remain structurally possible draw on this work.

Leike, J., et al. (2017). "AI Safety Gridworlds." arXiv:1711.09883.

Relevance: Provides benchmark environments for testing safe exploration and side-effect avoidance. CMHA V7's synthetic validation stage (Stage I) is methodologically related.

